{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0058","statement":"The paper addresses KBP slot filling, which extracts relation information from newswire documents for knowledge base construction.","source_span":"the task of KBP slot filling -- extracting relation information from newswire documents for knowledge base construction"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0058","statement":"The pipeline employs Relational Dependency Networks (RDNs) to learn linguistic patterns for relation extraction.","source_span":"employs Relational Dependency Networks (RDNs) to learn linguistic patterns for relation extraction"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0058","statement":"The system incorporates weak supervision, word2vec features, joint learning, and human advice as components.","source_span":"weak supervision, word2vec features, joint learning and the use of human advice, can be incorporated in this relational framework"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0058","statement":"The different components are evaluated on the benchmark KBP 2015 task.","source_span":"evaluate the different components in the benchmark KBP 2015 task"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0058","statement":"The KBP relation extraction task is commonly decomposed into two subtasks: entity linking and slot filling.","source_span":"entity linking, in which entities are linked to already identified identities within the document or to entities in the existing KB, and slot filling, which identifies certain attributes about a target entity"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0058","statement":"The authors learn on a set of 14 relations.","source_span":"present the set of 14 relations that we learn on"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0058","statement":"Raw text is processed using the Stanford CoreNLP Toolkit to extract parts-of-speech, word lemmas, parse trees, dependency graphs, and named-entity recognition information.","source_span":"Raw text is processed using the Stanford CoreNLP Toolkit to extract parts-of-speech, word lemmas, etc. as well as generate parse trees, dependency graphs and named-entity recognition information"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0058","statement":"The system uses word vectors from Stanford and Google along with expert-selected words related to the target relations, such as 'father' and 'mother', or 'devout', 'convert', and 'follow'.","source_span":"We use word vectors from Stanford and Google along with a few specific words that, experts believe, are related to the relations learned. For example, we include words such as \u201cfather\u201d and \u201cmother\u201d... or \u201cdevout\u201d,\u201cconvert\u201d, and \u201cfollow\u201d"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0058","statement":"The two weak supervision approaches used are distant supervision and knowledge-based weak supervision, following Soni et al. akbc16.","source_span":"we employ two approaches for generating weakly supervised examples \u2013 distant supervision and knowledge-based weak supervision"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0058","statement":"Distant supervision uses three data sources: Never Ending Language Learner (NELL), Wikipedia Infoboxes, and Freebase.","source_span":"we use three data sources \u2013 Never Ending Language Learner (NELL) BIBREF4 , Wikipedia Infoboxes and Freebase"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0058","statement":"A surprising finding was that weak supervision is not as useful as expected and word2vec features are not as predictive as other domain-specific features.","source_span":"weak supervision is not as useful as expected and word2vec features are not as predictive as the other domain-specific features"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0058","statement":"Further annotation of KBP documents with gold standard labels beyond a few hundred documents is prohibitively expensive.","source_span":"further annotation is prohibitively expensive beyond a few hundred documents"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0058","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What task does this paper's RDN-based pipeline address, and what technique does it use to learn linguistic patterns for relation extraction?","answer":"The paper addresses KBP slot filling, which extracts relation information from newswire documents for knowledge base construction. It uses Relational Dependency Networks (RDNs) to learn the linguistic patterns for relation extraction."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0058","fact_refs":[3,5],"question":"On which benchmark task is the system evaluated, and how many relations do the authors learn on?","answer":"The system is evaluated on the benchmark KBP 2015 task, and the authors learn on a set of 14 relations."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0058","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How is the KBP relation extraction task typically decomposed into subtasks?","answer":"It is commonly decomposed into entity linking, where entities are linked to identities within the document or existing KB, and slot filling, which identifies certain attributes about a target entity."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0058","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What NLP toolkit is used to process raw text, and what features does it extract?","answer":"The Stanford CoreNLP Toolkit is used to process raw text, extracting parts-of-speech, word lemmas, parse trees, dependency graphs, and named-entity recognition information."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0058","fact_refs":[8,9],"question":"What are the two weak supervision approaches used, and which data sources are used for distant supervision?","answer":"The two approaches are distant supervision and knowledge-based weak supervision, following Soni et al. akbc16. Distant supervision uses three data sources: NELL, Wikipedia Infoboxes, and Freebase."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0058","fact_refs":[10],"question":"What surprising result did the authors find regarding weak supervision and word2vec features?","answer":"They found that weak supervision was not as useful as expected, and word2vec features were not as predictive as other domain-specific features."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0058","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What kinds of example words did the authors add as features based on expert advice, and for which relations?","answer":"They included words like 'father' and 'mother' inspired by a parent relation, and words like 'devout', 'convert', and 'follow' for another relation, selected via high similarity in the word2vec embedding space."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0059","statement":"fastText can train on more than one billion words in less than ten minutes using a standard multicore CPU.","source_span":"We can train fastText on more than one billion words in less than ten minutes using a standard multicore~CPU"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0059","statement":"fastText can classify half a million sentences among 312K classes in less than a minute.","source_span":"classify half a million sentences among~312K classes in less than a minute"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0059","statement":"fastText is often on par with deep learning classifiers in accuracy while being many orders of magnitude faster for training and evaluation.","source_span":"our fast text classifier fastText is often on par with deep learning classifiers in terms of accuracy, and many orders of magnitude faster for training and evaluation"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0059","statement":"In the model architecture, word representations are averaged into a text representation which is fed to a linear classifier using a softmax function.","source_span":"The word representations are then averaged into a text representation, which is in turn fed to a linear classifier... We use the softmax function $f$ to compute the probability distribution over the predefined classes"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0059","statement":"Without hierarchical softmax, computing the linear classifier has computational complexity O(kh), where k is the number of classes and h is the dimension of the text representation.","source_span":"the computational complexity is $O(kh)$ where $k$ is the number of classes and $h$ the dimension of the text representation"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0059","statement":"Using hierarchical softmax based on a Huffman coding tree reduces training computational complexity to O(h log2(k)).","source_span":"we use a hierarchical softmax BIBREF15 based on the Huffman coding tree BIBREF10... the computational complexity drops to $O(h\\log _2(k))$"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0059","statement":"The approach can be extended to compute the T-top targets at a cost of O(log(T)) using a binary heap.","source_span":"This approach is further extended to compute the $T$ -top targets at the cost of $O(\\log (T))$ , using a binary heap"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0059","statement":"The hashing trick for n-grams uses 10M bins if only bigrams are used, and 100M bins otherwise.","source_span":"using the hashing trick BIBREF16 with the same hashing function as in mikolov2011strategies and 10M bins if we only used bigrams, and 100M otherwise"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0059","statement":"fastText is evaluated on two tasks: tag prediction and sentiment analysis.","source_span":"We evaluate the quality of our approach fastText on two different tasks, namely tag prediction and sentiment analysis"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0059","statement":"The model is trained asynchronously on multiple CPUs using stochastic gradient descent with a linearly decaying learning rate.","source_span":"This model is trained asynchronously on multiple CPUs using stochastic gradient descent and a linearly decaying learning rate"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0059","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"How fast can fastText train and classify text according to the paper?","answer":"fastText can train on more than one billion words in less than ten minutes on a standard multicore CPU, and classify half a million sentences among 312K classes in less than a minute."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0059","fact_refs":[2],"question":"How does fastText's accuracy and speed compare to deep learning classifiers?","answer":"fastText is often on par with deep learning classifiers in accuracy, while being many orders of magnitude faster for training and evaluation."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0059","fact_refs":[4,5],"question":"How does hierarchical softmax change the computational complexity of computing the linear classifier during training?","answer":"Without hierarchical softmax, the complexity is O(kh), where k is the number of classes and h is the text representation dimension. Using a Huffman-tree-based hierarchical softmax reduces this to O(h log2(k))."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0059","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What is the cost of computing the T-top target classes using the hierarchical softmax approach?","answer":"Computing the T-top targets costs O(log(T)), achieved using a binary heap."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0059","fact_refs":[7],"question":"How many hash bins does fastText use for n-gram features depending on whether only bigrams are used?","answer":"fastText uses 10M bins if only bigrams are used, and 100M bins otherwise, via the hashing trick."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0059","fact_refs":[8],"question":"On which two tasks is fastText evaluated in the paper?","answer":"fastText is evaluated on tag prediction and sentiment analysis."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0060","statement":"Traditional semantic parsers map language onto compositional, executable queries in a fixed schema.","source_span":"Traditional semantic parsers map language onto compositional, executable queries in a fixed schema"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0060","statement":"The example query for 'Who is the president of the United States?' executed against Freebase returns Barack Obama.","source_span":"the phrase \u201cWho is the president of the United States?\u201d might be mapped onto the query $\\lambda (x).$ $\\textsc {/government/president\\_of}$ ( $x$ , $\\textsc {USA}$ ), which, when executed against Freebase BIBREF2 , returns $\\textsc {Barack Obama}$"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0060","statement":"Open vocabulary semantic parsing methods replace a formal KB with a probabilistic database learned from a text corpus.","source_span":"methods for open vocabulary semantic parsing, which replace a formal KB with a probabilistic database learned from a text corpus"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0060","statement":"The paper's approach improves mean average precision on the open-domain question answering task by over 120%.","source_span":"we improve mean average precision on this task by over 120%"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0060","statement":"The current state-of-the-art model for open vocabulary semantic parsing was introduced by Krishnamurthy and Mitchell.","source_span":"the current state-of-the-art model for open vocabulary semantic parsing, introduced by Krishnamurthy and Mitchell krishnamurthy-2015-semparse-open-vocabulary"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0060","statement":"The matrix factorization model has two sets of parameters: a k-dimensional embedding theta for each category or relation, and a k-dimensional embedding phi for each entity or entity pair.","source_span":"each category or relation has a learned $k$ -dimensional embedding $\\theta $ , and each entity or entity pair has a learned $k$ -dimensional embedding $\\phi $"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0060","statement":"The probability assigned to a predicate instance is the sigmoided inner product of the corresponding predicate and entity embeddings.","source_span":"The probability of a predicate instance is the sigmoided inner product of the corresponding predicate and entity embeddings"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0060","statement":"The theta and phi parameters are learned using a query ranking objective that ranks entities observed in a logical form's denotation above unobserved entities.","source_span":"The parameters $\\theta $ and $\\phi $ are learned using a query ranking objective that optimizes them to rank entities observed in the denotation of a logical form above unobserved entities"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0060","fact_refs":[0],"question":"How do traditional semantic parsers map natural language, according to this paper?","answer":"Traditional semantic parsers map language onto compositional, executable queries in a fixed schema, which can be executed against a knowledge base."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0060","fact_refs":[1],"question":"In the paper's example, what entity does executing the mapped query for 'Who is the president of the United States?' against Freebase return?","answer":"It returns Barack Obama."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0060","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What do open vocabulary semantic parsing methods use in place of a formal knowledge base?","answer":"They replace a formal KB with a probabilistic database learned from a text corpus."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0060","fact_refs":[3],"question":"By how much does the paper's approach improve mean average precision on the open-domain question answering task?","answer":"It improves mean average precision on the task by over 120%."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0060","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Who introduced the state-of-the-art model for open vocabulary semantic parsing that this paper builds on?","answer":"Krishnamurthy and Mitchell introduced this state-of-the-art open vocabulary semantic parsing model."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0060","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What are the two sets of learned parameters in the matrix factorization approach used for open vocabulary semantic parsing?","answer":"Each category or relation has a learned k-dimensional embedding theta, and each entity or entity pair has a learned k-dimensional embedding phi."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0060","fact_refs":[6],"question":"How is the probability of a predicate instance computed in this matrix factorization model?","answer":"It is computed as the sigmoided inner product of the corresponding predicate embedding and entity embedding."}
